  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge continues to retreat towards the east, while\nupper-level troughing digs in along the west coast. A narrow plume\nof moisture aloft extends from SE Arizona into north-central New\nMexico, but very dry air in the mid and upper levels remains\nentrenched across most of west Texas into south-central New\nMexico. Low level moisture is lacking as well, and to see PWAT \nvalues much higher than the 1 inch mark, you'll need to head west\nof the Arizona state line, where an overnight Gulf Surge advected\nin plenty of low level moisture.\n\nFor us, expect widely scattered storms over the Black Range and\nGila through early this evening. perhaps sneaking into the higher\nelevations of the NM Bootheel as well. \n\nCluster of showers and thunderstorms in Sonora and Arizona will\nlift north and northeast towards the Four Corners overnight into\nSunday morning, ahead of the troughing. West winds tonight and\nearly Monday will advect some low level moisture into the lowlands\nfrom Arizona. Expect a modest uptick in thunderstorm coverage on\nMonday afternoon and evening as weak disturbances associated with\nthe troughing lift into the area, and broad upper-level diffluence\ndevelops ahead of the trough.\n\nFor what it's worth, the 18Z HRRR has a more pronounced uptick in\nthunderstorms over the south-central lowlands Monday night,\nseemingly aided by a low level convergence zone that sets up along\nthe Rio Grande. Worth watching, but it's hard to get hopes up. \n\nOverall better moisture lurks on Tuesday, but without as much\ninstability or lift, and then the ridge looks to build a western\nsecondary axis back over us for Wednesday. Things look more\noptimistic for later in the week as west coast troughing tries to\nimpinge on the western edge of the ridge again. Meanwhile, a weak\ntropical system in the Gulf looks to stay well to our east, thanks\nto the westward limb of the ridge.",
